                The Convolutional Manifold Problem
            </h3>
            
            <p>
                How did a mechanism that scored 91.49% on MNIST score 0.0% on CIFAR-10? The data reveals a profound lesson in deep learning mechanics. 
            </p>

            <div class="discovery-box">
                <strong class="text-orange-500 uppercase tracking-widest text-xs block mb-2">Tethering the Wrong End of the Manifold</strong>
                <p class="m-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-300">
                    The math of our JVP operator calculated the directional derivative of the network's outputs with respect to its <em>raw input pixels</em>. On a simple MLP mapping MNIST pixels, constraining this surface tension worked perfectly. However, CIFAR-10 requires a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). 
                    <br><br>
                    CNNs rely on hierarchical feature extraction (edges $\rightarrow$ shapes $\rightarrow$ objects). By applying the tether to the raw input pixels, we only constrained the absolute lowest layer. The network simply ignored the tether, completely scrambled its deep convolutional filters to learn Task B, and destroyed the feature extractors required for Task A while technically satisfying the pixel-level gradient constraint. 
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-gray-400 italic mt-8 border-l-4 border-red-900 pl-4 bg-red-900/10 py-3 pr-3 rounded-r-lg">
                "The preregistration trap worked flawlessly. It prevented post-hoc victory language and exposed the conceptual overfitting to MNIST. A scientific architecture must be able to lose in order to evolve. We now know that to constrain a deep convolutional manifold, the tether must be applied to the latent feature space, not the input pixels."
            </p>
